<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People Power Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EDSA Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People Power Revolution was a peaceful popular uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> on 22–25 February 1986 that ended the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a>. After a disputed presidential election, military rebels and huge crowds gathered on Epifanio de los Santos Avenue (EDSA) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, backed by the Catholic Church. Marcos fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corazon-aquino">Corazon Aquino</a> became president, restoring democratic government.</p></div>
